      { "text": "Dengue fever", "explanation": "Correct. The clinical and epidemiologic picture is the textbook presentation of dengue fever: Southeast Asian travel exposure, 5-day incubation, fever, severe myalgia/arthralgia described as 'break-bone fever,' retro-orbital pain, maculopapular rash, leukopenia, and thrombocytopenia with mild transaminitis. Dengue is endemic throughout Southeast Asia and increasingly in the Americas (including parts of the US — Florida, Texas, California). The Aedes aegypti vector also carries Zika, yellow fever, and chikungunya — co-infection is possible. Diagnosis is by RT-PCR (acute phase) or NS1 antigen detection; serology for IgM/IgG can also be used but cross-reactivity with other flaviviruses can complicate. Treatment is supportive — careful fluid management is critical (avoid both under- and over-resuscitation as the leak resolves). The key complication to watch for is progression to dengue hemorrhagic fever, especially if this is a secondary dengue infection in someone with prior infection by a different serotype (antibody-dependent enhancement). Outpatient management is appropriate for mild cases without warning signs; hospitalization for severe cases or those with vascular leak indicators." }
